Z||000002||mindscape||07-21-2001||02:30 AM||||So, I have a 440T4 from a 1987 Buick Electra Park Avenue.  I've done a search and read that a couple of hole must be drilled.  Where are you guys making new holes/?||||reg||5
Z||000003||rockcrawl||07-21-2001||07:39 AM|||| <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP00972.JPG"> <P>That's a picture of the drivers side rear of the cradle. The holes are slotted to allow the stock Fiero mount to sit in the proper position for the 4 speed bracket. The little alignment nipple on the mount needs to be ground off, or a hole drilled in the bracket to line up with the nipple<P>The drivers side output shaft has 35 splines, the Fiero axle has 34. You need to use a tripod from a 4 speed. The passenger side axle has the correct splines, but it will be too long.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mindscape||07-21-2001||10:07 PM||||How long did you elongate the holes?  I'm doing things the hard way.  Trans... then motor...  because I don't have a way to do it otherwise.<P>Which set of holes did you use on the front?  Are the trans mounts different?<P>Thanks!<p>[This message has been edited by mindscape (edited 07-21-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000005||mindscape||07-21-2001||10:12 PM||||Okay, I'm confused... the hols that are slotted are on the rear (trunk side).  The emergency brake cable is throwing me off.  I take they are in different places 88 vs. other models?||||reg||5
Z||000006||KRMFiero||07-21-2001||10:35 PM||||Didnt you say 89 Ponitac 6000 Automatic Axles (with Light DUty Brakes) were a direct fit?  I was also told with frotn and rear 6000 trans mounts were a direct fit?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||07-22-2001||02:52 PM||||Mindscape,<BR> I didn't realize you were working with an '88. The '88 brake cables are in a different location and the cradle looks a bit different. I don't have an '88 cradle here to look at, but I'm pretty sure the holes can be slotted in the same way. The holes are slotted about two inches. I usually go farther than I need to just to be sure there is plenty of room for adjustment to get the trans straight. On the front side you will use the set of holes toward the center of the car. You will use the brackets that came with the Buick. You can use the '87 Park Ave or '84-'86 Fiero rubbers, they are the same front and rear. Once you get all the parts together and see how well it fits, you will be amazed.<P>Kyle,<BR> Yes the 89 Ponitac 6000 four speed automatic axles (with Light Duty Brakes) will fit. I was just trying to explain why the Fiero axles won't work. The 6000 mounts and brackets will fit also, but you still need to slot the rear holes to get it sitting straight.||||reg||1
Z||000008||mindscape||07-24-2001||01:10 AM||||Rockcrawl, elongated the holes on the rear of the cradle today, and it looks like it will work.  However, I could not use the Buick bracket.  Instead I used one from a Transport; bracket mount is offset towards driver side.  But, I still have an axle too long on the passenger side.  Suppose I shall have words with someone.<P>BTW, I have pictures if anyone is interested.||||reg||6
