Z||000010||KRMFiero||07-24-2001||06:47 AM||||As soon as Summer school ends for me Ill tell you how the TH440T4 mounts with Ponitac 6000 parts, my axles are comming in tommrow, mounts i am having trouble with because autozone and other local places do not offer them, so i am going to a GM dealer Friday, BTW Im doing the whole 4.5/440T swap  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||onfire||07-24-2001||01:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>As soon as Summer school ends for me Ill tell you how the TH440T4 mounts with Ponitac 6000 parts, my axles are comming in tommrow, mounts i am having trouble with because autozone and other local places do not offer them, so i am going to a GM dealer Friday, BTW Im doing the whole 4.5/440T swap   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That would be great. I can't wait to get started on mine. Right now it needs brakes bad, and my friend has some 17inch wheels and tires for sale cheap. Plus a few other things that need to be taken care before I get to the engine. If everything goes well the car should be about 85% where I want it by next summer. All that should be left is interior work and body work.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000012||rockcrawl||07-24-2001||04:10 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>But, I still have an axle too long on the passenger side. Suppose I shall have words with someone.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>What axle are you using? If you got it from a salvage yard, it's very possible they gave you the wrong one. I tried to get some used ones once, but when neither one of them was correct, I just ordered new ones.||||reg||1
Z||000013||mindscape||07-24-2001||04:22 PM||||What is the overall length of the axle compressed on the passenger side. The one I had madeup appears to be too long, i.e., installed it is bottomed out.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||pherder||07-24-2001||05:18 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mindscape:<BR><B>What is the overall length of the axle compressed on the passenger side. The one I had madeup appears to be too long, i.e., installed it is bottomed out.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>IAW a web site someone else pointed out to me, the axles we need for the Fiero install of the 440T4 AOD is 646 mm (25.43") passenger side and 532 mm (20.94") driver side.<P>These measurements came from  <A HREF="http://www.automotive-axles.com/pontiac_axles_by_model_0.html" TARGET=_blank>www.automotive-axles.com/pontiac_axles_by_model_0.html</A> <P>Be advised, these are the HD axles but should be the same length.<P>The Fiero's are at  <A HREF="http://www.automotive-axles.com/pontiac_axles_by_model_12.html" TARGET=_blank>www.automotive-axles.com/pontiac_axles_by_model_12.html</A> <P>Paul<p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 07-24-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||07-24-2001||06:23 PM||||The ones Paul posted are the correct length, but they do have the larger outer joints as he stated. I assume the lengths they list are compressed. The small outboard is 12mm shorter than the large one. Your overall passenger side axle length should be 634mm. So you have an axle that's 47mm shorter than a Fiero axle. ||||reg||1
Z||000016||mindscape||07-26-2001||01:05 PM||||Which lines on the 440T4 are what?  Out? In?<P>Same for the Fiero, which line is what?  To? From?<P>Have axles installed.  Using a Rockford 2461 with a starting length of 25 and 7/8 inches with the Fiero manual end the length is about 25 1/2 inches.||||reg||5
Z||000017||isthiswhereiputausername?||07-26-2001||01:08 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by KRMFiero:<BR><B>Didnt you say 89 Ponitac 6000 Automatic Axles (with Light DUty Brakes) were a direct fit?  I was also told with frotn and rear 6000 trans mounts were a direct fit?<P>Kyle</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>AS far as I know, Yes they are. <P>I have Buick skylark axles and mounts on mine now.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||mindscape||07-29-2001||01:09 AM||||Rockcrawl, how about a picture of one of your installs?  I'm curious how far over the rear mount sits.  On my 1988 GT I had to cut the side of the mount off to get it to fit and slot the rear upper hole to the limit where the cradle starts to slope down.<P>How did you post your picture?||||reg||1
Z||000019||rockcrawl||07-29-2001||07:17 AM||||I'll take some pics when I get home from the salveage yard today||||reg||1
Z||000020||rockcrawl||07-29-2001||08:43 PM||||here's a pic taken from underneath the drivers side rear of the car. The center of the studs are 1.25" from the outside edge of the original holes. This is an '84-'87. I hope that makes sense.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01283.JPG"> <BR>The next picture is an '88 cradle, and below that is a rusty '87.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01285.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01286.JPG"> <BR> To move the hole over 1.25" on the '88 would put it right at the edge of where it starts dropping off. I would put it as close as possible to the edge, then also slot the hole in the bracket that bolts to the trans for a little extra adjustment. This isn't necesary on the pre-'88 because there is more flat area where the holes are.<P>Next is a pic of the tubes/hoses coming out of the trans. They originally pointed straight forward but I bent them out toward the side. <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01289.JPG"> <P>The top hose connects to the tube on the Fiero that points downward, and the bottom hose connects to the tube pointing back. Here's a rather confusing pic of that.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01291.JPG"> <P>That should pump the hot fluid into the top port of the cooler in the radiator. It's not really too critical to get them right because the radaitor is a crossflow design, and the entire trans cooler section is in the hot tank. It shouldn't make much difference which way they're hooked up.<P>Hope that helps.<BR>Jon||||reg||1
Z||000021||mindscape||08-01-2001||01:52 AM||||Well, I have her back together.  But, now let's wrestle with the shifter issue.  I flipped the shifter arm around and moved the bracket.  Seems that on my 1988 GT I can only get to 4th (OD) and no lower.<P>What points would you set this critter to shift at?<P>Also, another 1988 note... I had to use an aftermarket modulator, the grey one that came with the unit would not fit because of the cooling tube||||reg||5
Z||000022||terryk||08-01-2001||02:01 PM||||Search for my 3800SC/4T60E posts for details.<P>You have to notch out more of the shift lever detent below 1st (the lever needs more range 1/4" more?) and you have to change the length of arm on the trans (change the ratio). <P>I finally built a reverser plate for the trans. The cable comes in on one side and the arm pivots and reverses the cable's direction. If the cable pulls, the trans lever is pushed and vice versa.||||reg||1
Z||000023||rockcrawl||08-01-2001||04:27 PM||||Here's how I handled the shifting situation. I flattened and shortened the arm on the trans. I reworked the cable mount so that it would be at the proper angle and distance from the arm. And like Terry suggested, I added an extra detent to the shifter for low gear. <BR> The reason you can't shift into any gear below fourth is because the arm is too long. If you are going to do the same thing I did, I'll measure the length of the arm to save you some headaches. Here are some pics without the cable installed. This is a V8, on a 2.8 the exhaust pipe might be in the way and prevent the arm from being flipped. Terry??<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01233.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/DCP01234.JPG"> <P>To clear the exhaust on a 3800, I extended the arm up about four inches. I put a door hinge bushing at the pivot point and ran a brace to the cable bracket to prevent lateral strain on the shaft. The bracketry isn't shown, but here's a pic of the arm to give you an idea. It works very smoothly.<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/dcp00991.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000024||terryk||08-01-2001||04:47 PM||||Mine was on a 3800SC and 4T60E. There was no way the arm could be reversed and select the gears. The crossover was in the way (sits lower over the trans side cover). The reverser was the only way I could handle it. The &lt;insert aftermarket Fiero company name here&gt; solution with the 9 in arm wasn't going to work either. <P>In your pics, it looks like you can slip under the crossover. In that case, it looks like a good solution.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 08-01-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000025||KRMFiero||08-01-2001||09:21 PM||||I havent tried/messuered yet but would stock 3 speed arm be the right size?<P>Kyle||||reg||1
Z||000026||mindscape||08-05-2001||03:38 PM||||What can be done about harsh reverse engage?||||reg||5
Z||000027||theogre||08-05-2001||04:44 PM||||Harsh reverse would usually indicate an internal problem in the tranny. <P>I've lost track of which tranny who used... If this is the van tranny then it's posible the gearing is quite low. There may not be anything you can do about a hard reverse. the gears may simply be so low that the torque is shoving things around.||||reg||1
Z||000028||mindscape||08-05-2001||09:12 PM||||I read somewhere on the net that with the  440T4 it can be caused by heavily oxidized fluid; something about replacing an internal part to fix.  However, I would like to attempt a fix before going through all the trouble of removing it.  I bought this critter from a salvage yard.||||reg||1
Z||000029||MinnGreenGT||08-06-2001||10:02 AM||||Hey guys... I'm running basically the same setup (440 in my '87) but never got around to wiring the tranny for final lockup.  Anyone happen to know offhand how to do this?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/grngt.jpg">  <P>Medium Metallic Green 87 GT<BR>3.1L High Output<BR>4 Spd OD Tranny<BR>Open (read: Loud) Exhaust<BR>and More!<BR>-Rob Bartlett||||reg||1
Z||000030||SteveJ||08-06-2001||10:26 AM||||Harsh shifts in the 440t4 are caused by the vacuum modulator - $10 <P>But that would apply to all the shifts not just reverse wouldn't it?||||reg||1
Z||000031||Jim Jimenez||08-07-2001||12:18 AM||||Since the subject has come up, What do you use as a vacuum source for the modulator line?<P>Engine is a stock 2.8 Been looking for a '85/6 A body to use as reference. Seems there are none left!!||||reg||10
Z||000032||mindscape||08-07-2001||01:34 AM||||My 440T4 clunks when downshifting from 2 to 1 and is harsh when going from park to reverse or from neutral to reverse.  Yes, I'm using an aftermarkert modulator.  It shifts nicely when driving; downshifts out on the road nicely; does not always clunk when coming to a stop when shifting 2 to 1.  Suppose I'll be going to a shop.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000033||mindscape||08-07-2001||01:39 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jim Jimenez:<BR><B>Since the subject has come up, What do you use as a vacuum source for the modulator line?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I'm using the larger line on the throttle body; the one that goes to the curse control.<P>Which one should I use?<P>I understand that if the modulator isn't adjusted correctly and the T.V. cable is not adjusted correctly it will cause this sort of behavior.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000034||SteveJ||08-07-2001||09:49 AM||||The modulator controls the harshness of the shifts. The TV cable controls at what speed the transmission shifts in moderate acceleration.<BR>When the TV cable is fully retracted it shifts at a very high speed. When adjusted according to the manual by pulling the cable out of the adjuster it lowers the shift speed. ( with the engine off you open the throttle all the way ) With my car I prefer an even lower shift speed so I pull the cable out two or three more clicks past that.<BR>My aftermarket modulator has an adjustment screw inside the vacuum feed nipple. You use a really small screwdriver. You can go all the way from a harsh banging shift to a total slippage of the shift. Either extreme is bad. <BR>Incidentally, if you block off the vacuum to the modulator and drive it a short distance you can verify the intactness of your transmission. All shifts slam into place. Very hard on the transmission. But it tells you all gears, modulators, valves, and so on are working its just the modulator that need adjusting or replacement. I don't recommend this for any car you think a lot of.||||reg||1
Z||000035||87GTZ34||08-07-2001||10:10 AM||||mindscape, There are a series of pressure reg, accumulator and boost valves in the 440T all of which can cause wierd symptoms when sticking. A couple of questions... what is your idle RPM when shifting? Have you properly adjusted the TV cable?  Also, find out from the yard as much about the car as you can... when did they get the car, when did they pull the trans, was the car running but wrecked (or junked) or was it sitting several years in a field?  Also, use a trans fluid additive to try to help clean up the internals.  Drive it for awhile then change the fluid again.  Follow Ogre's que on draining fluid from the torque converter although I flush from the cooler return line by the trans axle to flush that as well. Also I would take it to a remote area, (or empty parking lot) and 'exercise' (not abuse)the tranny for an hour or so.  rev to 1, 1 to rev, rev to 2, 2 to rev, rev to dr, dr to rev etc. while getting it good and hot, to help loosen and wear away deposits.  One thing you can do to diagnose it is to hook up a trans pressure gauge and check pressures at their respective locations in the respective gears.  Unless you know someone you can trust, a trans shop will see major dollar signs and suggest overhaul.<BR>I only have the GM trans manuals for the electronic transmissions or I would give you pressures.  I have chiltons for the non electronics but that is a POS (like not having a manual at all).  I want to put a non-electronic 4spd in my 2nd car so I will have to break down and buy one as I rebuild them before I put them in the car.<BR>GL!!!<p>[This message has been edited by 87GTZ34 (edited 08-07-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000036||theogre||08-07-2001||07:25 PM||||I don't have a whole lot of stuff on the 440t4... What I do have says it's really picky about just how the modulator and TV cable are adjusted. These two are used to set all forward shift points.<P>The TV does effect line pressure so I'd suspect the TV cable more than the modulator for reverse.<P>The one thing you don't want to be doing to these is randomly adjusting things. You need a manual and a pressure gauge to get even close to the right settings.<P>Also there are a bunch of GM TSB's on the 4 speeds. one or more of them may apply to your tranny.||||reg||1
